  "limitations": [
    {
      "id": "AMBIGUITY_UNTESTED",
      "statement": "The two derivations agreed, but re-running all 38 under the alternative reading of the opaque-call ambiguity changes membership on 0 of 38. The corpus does not exercise the specification's underdetermined regions, so agreement is evidence about the mechanical core of the rules and none about the unclear parts."
    },
    {
      "id": "TRIVIAL_ROWS_IN_THE_DENOMINATOR",
      "statement": "Eight of the 38 rows contribute two nodes or fewer and one edge or fewer, and three of them are the queries Round B recorded as not doing what they claim: M7-Q-C03-EXEC is a criterion with no execution input so its slice is itself, M7-Q-C09-DATA has 24 Gets and no Set so three of four data rules never fire, and M7-Q-C09-EXEC stops at depth one behind an opaque call. Those three contribute 27 of the 266 nodes, 10.2 per cent of the node total. An exact-match rate of 38/38 therefore counts rows that exercise very little, and the aggregate is not a difficulty-weighted measure."
    },
    {
      "id": "ADJUDICATOR_WAS_A_PARTY",
      "statement": "The controller produced Round B and also adjudicated. At zero disagreements nothing turned on it; at one it would have been disqualifying."
    },
    {
      "id": "INHERITED_TRUTH_NOT_REDERIVED",
      "statement": "Five ground-truth artefacts inherited from M4 and M5 are independent_review, not re-derivation \u2014 a second party checked an answer it could see. They are not measured in this report, and their weaker model must not be read across."
    },
    {
      "id": "TOP_BAND_DATA_THIN",
      "statement": "The top-band data slice is two nodes because every member in that generated graph is set exactly once. Top-band data coverage is not established, whatever the aggregate says."
    },
    {
      "id": "SHARED_MISREADING_UNRULED_OUT",
      "statement": "Both derivations are language models reading the same frozen text. A phrasing that misleads one may mislead both identically, and nothing in this milestone rules that out. Two derivations with one adjudicator cannot catch a misreading they share."
    }
  ],
